                "wrongAnswerMessage": "These are actually distinct categories. Zoonoses are defined by their transmission route — from vertebrate animals to humans — and can infect healthy people. Opportunistic infections exploit weakened immunity regardless of where the pathogen comes from. The two concepts overlap but are not the same.",
                "correctAnswerMessage": "Correct. These are distinct categories. Zoonoses are defined by their animal-to-human transmission route and can infect fully immunocompetent individuals. Opportunistic infections are defined by the host's compromised immune status, regardless of the pathogen's source. Some infections such as toxoplasmosis are both, but the categories themselves are fundamentally different.",
