          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A sewer worker presents with high fever, jaundice, and acute renal failure — the</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A sewer worker presents with high fever, jaundice, and acute renal failure — the classic triad of Weil's disease. Which laboratory investigation is the gold standard for confirming the diagnosis of leptospirosis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Dark-field microscopy of blood</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Dark-field microscopy can detect motile spirochaetes, but it is prone to artefacts and has low sensitivity. It is a supportive investigation, not the gold standard.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Microscopic agglutination test (MAT)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The MAT detects antibodies against specific Leptospira serovars and is the internationally recognised reference standard for serological confirmation of leptospirosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Blood culture in EMJH medium</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Blood culture can grow Leptospira but takes 2–4 weeks and works best in the first week of illness. It is not the gold standard for confirmation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Widal agglutination test</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The Widal test detects antibodies against Salmonella typhi, not Leptospira. It is used for typhoid fever diagnosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is the microscopic agglutination test (MAT). While dark-field microscopy and blood culture are useful, MAT is the internationally recognised gold standard for serological confirmation of leptospirosis."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The microscopic agglutination test (MAT) remains the gold standard serological test for leptospirosis, detecting serovar-specific antibodies with high specificity."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
