          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>An HIV-positive patient with a CD4 count of 80 cells/μL presents with headache, </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>An HIV-positive patient with a CD4 count of 80 cells/μL presents with headache, neck stiffness, and altered sensorium. India ink staining of the cerebrospinal fluid reveals yeast cells surrounded by a clear halo. Which opportunistic infection is the most likely diagnosis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pneumocystis pneumonia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pneumocystis pneumonia typically presents with dry cough, dyspnoea, and bilateral interstitial infiltrates — not meningitis. It is associated with CD4 counts below 200.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Toxoplasma encephalitis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Toxoplasma encephalitis presents with ring-enhancing lesions in the basal ganglia on MRI, with seizures and focal deficits. India ink staining would not detect Toxoplasma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cryptococcal meningitis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Cryptococcus neoformans causes subacute meningitis in severely immunocompromised patients (CD4 &lt; 100). The thick polysaccharide capsule creates the characteristic clear halo on India ink staining of CSF.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Candida oesophagitis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Candida oesophagitis presents with odynophagia and dysphagia, not meningitis. It manifests as white plaques in the oesophagus, typically at CD4 below 200.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is cryptococcal meningitis. At a CD4 count of 80, this patient is at risk for infections that appear below 100. The India ink finding of yeast cells with a clear halo is characteristic of Cryptococcus neoformans."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent. Cryptococcal meningitis is a classic opportunistic infection at CD4 < 100, diagnosed by the pathognomonic India ink appearance of encapsulated yeast in CSF."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
