          "label": "<p>Which mechanism best explains how high-risk HPV types 16 and 18 drive the develo</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which mechanism best explains how high-risk HPV types 16 and 18 drive the development of cervical carcinoma?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Integration of viral DNA into the host genome and activation of cellular oncogenes via the HBx protein</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This describes the oncogenic mechanism of hepatitis B virus, not HPV. HBx is a hepatitis B–specific oncoprotein that activates host oncogenes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chronic inflammation and viral protein interference with p53, without any DNA integration stage</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This describes the mechanism of hepatitis C virus, an RNA virus that drives hepatocellular carcinoma through chronic inflammation — not HPV's mechanism.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Infection of B lymphocytes leading to their immortalisation and uncontrolled proliferation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This describes the oncogenic mechanism of Epstein-Barr virus, which infects B cells and is linked to Burkitt's lymphoma and nasopharyngeal carcinoma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Oncoprotein E6 degrades p53 while E7 inactivates the retinoblastoma protein, disabling cell-cycle checkpoints</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. HPV E6 targets p53, the 'guardian of the genome', for degradation, removing the apoptotic brake. E7 inactivates Rb, releasing E2F and pushing the cell cycle forward unchecked — together driving carcinogenesis over 10–20 years.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is E6 degrading p53 and E7 inactivating Rb. Together these two oncoproteins disable the cell's main tumour-suppressor checkpoints, leading to uncontrolled proliferation and eventually invasive cervical carcinoma."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The E6–p53 and E7–Rb interactions are the core molecular mechanism by which high-risk HPV types drive cervical carcinogenesis, preventable today with vaccines like Gardasil-9 and CERVAVAC."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
